# Local-first architecture and competitive analysis for hackathon project

Sat, 21 Nov 26

## Architecture: Local-First Approach

- Core design decision: everything runs locally
  - Key differentiator from other hackathon teams
  - Avoids cloud data privacy issues entirely
  - One judge runs CVS (healthcare), so privacy angle is well-timed
- Cloud extension planned as bonus feature
  - Masks/redacts data before sending to any cloud API
  - Supports firms that already pay for cloud models and want to use them

## Ticket Triage and Classification

- Goal: triage tickets into categories (low, medium, critical), not solve them
  - Challenge spec is about surfacing solutions, not building fixes
  - Actual ticket resolution left to the company’s own tools and interface
- Urgency classification via keyword probability
  - If 90%+ of a specific word’s usage maps to “critical,” auto-flag it
- Autonomous ticket resolution flagged as stretch/theoretical
  - Can demo the concept without building it out fully
- Input validation: text-based claim checks yes, image-based no

## Competitive Intelligence

- Pulled all public GitHub repos from concurrent GDG hackathons
  - Filtered by most recent commits to find active teams
  - Zipped repos and fed to Claude for synthesis
  - Output: .md peer review summary, shared to Discord
- Other concurrent hackathons found: Germany and Hungary
  - Hungarian repos also being reviewed
  - Idea floated to reach out to German team for collaboration

## UI and Product Experience

- Current UI: ticket interface on the right side, expandable
  - Recommendation: replace transparent bars in “where incidents come from” section (feels claustrophobic)
  - Reference sites: Carvana, CarMax (chat widget in bottom corner)
- Proposed UX flow:
  - External: chat popup in bottom-right corner for customer-facing queries
  - Internal: operator/agent-facing view
  - Emails and chat messages routed to the same backend pipeline
- FAQ/popular topics section suggested (à la Carvana)
  - Could cache frequent questions to reduce latency
- Website as proof-of-concept for judges, offer to build it out

